---
import PhotoPlaceholder from '../PhotoPlaceholder.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../PullQuote.astro';
import SectionHead from '../SectionHead.astro';
---

<section class="band-ink section">
  <SectionHead num="14 / 14 — Civic frame" heading="The dishwasher and the dean." />

  <div class="container-page">
    <PhotoPlaceholder
      variant="ink"
      description="A BRT car at midmorning. A casino exec in a suit. A teacher with a tote of papers. A line cook in chef's whites. A teenager. A tourist. Same ride."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        Transit isn't something poor people have to use. Done right, it's something a city makes so
        good that everyone wants to use it. The 14th-busiest bus system in the country is already in
        this Valley — carrying nurses, college students, dishwashers, security officers,
        grandmothers, tourists. The won version of this adds a casino VP, a chemistry teacher, a
        wedding party headed downtown, a Saturday worth of teenagers going to a movie. The
        dishwasher and the dean on the same BRT car, with no need for either of them to explain
        themselves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <PullQuote cite="Willie Chalmers III, Founder">
      We want public transportation to be seen not just as something <em>"only poor people use,"</em
      > but as part of everyday life for a real share of Las&nbsp;Vegas.
    </PullQuote>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        The cultural fight matters as much as the funding fight, and possibly more. As long as
        transit is something Las&nbsp;Vegans are embarrassed to admit they use, the political case
        for building more of it stays harder than it should be. Once it is something Las&nbsp;Vegans
        choose, freely, because it's actually good — the political case becomes self-reinforcing.
        Phoenix reached that turn. Salt Lake reached that turn. Albuquerque is reaching it now. We
        can too.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the work LVBT exists to do, alongside the funding fights and the project advocacy
        and the coalition-building. Not just buses, but a Valley people are proud to share a ride
        with.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="vision-tail">The dishwasher and the dean. On the same ride.</p>
  </div>
</section>
